            <ArticleTitle>Pre-hospital evaluation of electrocardiographic grade 3 ischemia predicts infarct progression and final infarct size in ST elevation myocardial infarction patients treated with primary percutaneous coronary intervention.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>556-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jelectrocard.2014.04.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-0736(14)00144-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">In STEMI, grade-3 ischemia (G3) on admission ECG predicts larger infarct size (IS) than grade-2 (G2). We evaluated whether pre-hospital G3 and its temporal behavior are associated with IS and salvage after pPCI.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In 401 STEMI patients, pre-hospital and pre-PCI ECGs were classified as G3 or G2. IS was assessed by single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) at 30days. In 245 patients, pre-PCI SPECT was available to determine myocardium at risk (MaR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">G3 criteria were met by 88, and G2 by 313 patients. G3 was independently associated with IS (p=0.006). With ST resolution (STR) group as a reference, G2-&gt;G2, G2-&gt;G3 and G3-&gt;G3 were associated with larger IS (B=4.4, p=0.004; B=5.4, p=0.01; B=10.2, p&lt;0.001, respectively), whereas G3-&gt;G2 was not. Salvage was similar between G3 and G2 on pre-hospital ECG if treated early, however lower for G3 when treated later (&gt;2.5h); 48% (35-78) vs 62% (40-87); p=0.04.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Development or persistence of G3 is associated with larger IS and less salvage, but decreasing grade G3-&gt;G2 was not.</AbstractText>
